00:09:11.184 --> 00:09:12.684
kind of this COVID strangeness,

00:09:12.684 --> 00:09:15.414
and then what needs to happen after COVID

00:09:15.414 --> 00:09:17.944
to make sure that the
damage isn't long lasting

00:09:17.944 --> 00:09:19.004
and what can you do at the federal level?

00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:20.094
That's what's always really interesting.

00:09:20.094 --> 00:09:22.574
What can the secretary of
education really do here?

00:09:22.574 --> 00:09:23.814
- Well, there's so much

00:09:24.852 --> 00:09:26.754
that that the next
administration has to do.

00:09:26.754 --> 00:09:29.084
And let me just give you a
couple of different things

00:09:29.084 --> 00:09:30.504
to think about.

00:09:30.504 --> 00:09:33.274
I will always start with pre-K

00:09:33.274 --> 00:09:35.534
that we have to get our
babies off to a good start.

00:09:35.534 --> 00:09:37.384
For me that's the best
investment we can make.

00:09:37.384 --> 00:09:38.217
We gotta stop.

00:09:38.217 --> 00:09:40.084
- Arne the producer saying,

00:09:40.084 --> 00:09:41.344
if you can tilt the camera a little bit,

00:09:41.344 --> 00:09:43.284
so I'd want to cut it down a little bit.

00:09:43.284 --> 00:09:44.634
You're getting cut off by your name.

00:09:44.634 --> 00:09:45.467
There you go, perfect, okay.

00:09:45.467 --> 00:09:46.924
Sorry, I didn't wanna.

00:09:46.924 --> 00:09:48.234
- Is that, is that okay?

00:09:48.234 --> 00:09:52.600
- Much, much, much better,
yes, sorry I didn't wanna.

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:54.340
- You got to work on the
technical side of this stuff.

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.490
- People are still
behind the scenes today.

00:09:57.924 --> 00:10:00.374
- I'd start with a massive
investment in pre-K

00:10:00.374 --> 00:10:01.694
and getting more and more children

00:10:01.694 --> 00:10:04.524
across the country ready for school.

00:10:04.524 --> 00:10:07.327
Secondly, we have to
close the digital divide.

00:10:08.174 --> 00:10:10.484
Next administration could
do that with the FCC.

00:10:10.484 --> 00:10:13.804
We gotta make access to devices, to wifi,

00:10:13.804 --> 00:10:17.494
to the internet, as
ubiquitous, as electricity

00:10:17.494 --> 00:10:20.014
and drinking water kids have
to be able to learn anything

00:10:20.014 --> 00:10:22.114
they want, anytime, anywhere,

00:10:22.114 --> 00:10:24.454
inner city communities, rural communities,

00:10:24.454 --> 00:10:27.044
suburban communities, native
American reservations.

00:10:27.044 --> 00:10:28.547
That has to be the norm.

00:10:29.694 --> 00:10:31.084
We have to think very differently

00:10:31.084 --> 00:10:34.064
as you and I have talked
about the school day,

00:10:34.064 --> 00:10:37.394
the school year, the
calendar having summer off

00:10:37.394 --> 00:10:38.664
for everybody doesn't make sense.

00:10:38.664 --> 00:10:40.794
We have a lot of kids
that need to catch up.

00:10:40.794 --> 00:10:42.764
You'll lengthening the time
which kids have a chance

00:10:42.764 --> 00:10:44.544
to learn is so important.

00:10:44.544 --> 00:10:47.314
I think we have to think
differently about the length

00:10:47.314 --> 00:10:49.454
of time we ask kids to go to school.

00:10:49.454 --> 00:10:52.774
We've had a kindergarten
through 12th grade model.

00:10:52.774 --> 00:10:54.604
That's worked well for
the last a hundred years.

00:10:54.604 --> 00:10:56.974
I don't think it's suited
well at all for the next five

00:10:56.974 --> 00:10:59.174
or 10 years, let alone the next 20 or 30.

00:10:59.174 --> 00:11:02.514
I think we have to move to
a pre-K through 14 model

00:11:02.514 --> 00:11:04.044
and make that the norm.

00:11:04.044 --> 00:11:05.264
And then finally, for those kids

00:11:05.264 --> 00:11:08.114
that have been most
impacted by the COVID slide

00:11:08.114 --> 00:11:10.344
and then summer slide,
and then may not be back

00:11:10.344 --> 00:11:11.784
at school this fall.

00:11:11.784 --> 00:11:14.284
And that can literally be a
couple million kids South.

00:11:14.284 --> 00:11:15.794
So these are not small numbers.

00:11:15.794 --> 00:11:18.554
I would love to see a
national tutoring initiative

00:11:18.554 --> 00:11:20.714
where we can work with
those children physically,

00:11:20.714 --> 00:11:23.804
virtually to try and help them catch up.

00:11:23.804 --> 00:11:25.654
We can't allow them to have a lost year

00:11:25.654 --> 00:11:28.084
'cause if we lose a year,
we may lose them forever.

00:11:28.084 --> 00:11:30.684
And so there's so much that
the next administration

00:11:30.684 --> 00:11:34.704
has to do work with
tremendous urgency invest.

00:11:34.704 --> 00:11:36.114
I would say a great deal.

00:11:36.114 --> 00:11:38.124
A great military is our best defense,

00:11:38.124 --> 00:11:39.334
but a great educational systems,

00:11:39.334 --> 00:11:40.954
our best offense as a country,

00:11:40.954 --> 00:11:42.984
we need an administration
that truly understands

00:11:42.984 --> 00:11:44.814
that we have to invest in kids

00:11:44.814 --> 00:11:45.994
and ultimately in our families

00:11:45.994 --> 00:11:47.744
and our communities in our country.

00:11:48.904 --> 00:11:50.984
- And I couldn't agree
more with pretty much

00:11:50.984 --> 00:11:52.334
everything you just said.

00:11:52.334 --> 00:11:54.814
I mean, just taking them one
by one the digital divide,

00:11:54.814 --> 00:11:56.884
that's one, you and I
have talked a lot about,

00:11:56.884 --> 00:11:58.944
obviously even to start
accessing Khan Academy

00:11:58.944 --> 00:12:00.464
and other resources, or just participating

00:12:00.464 --> 00:12:03.464
in digital learning or
distance learning, you need it.

00:12:03.464 --> 00:12:07.464
What's your sense of what
it would take to get,

00:12:07.464 --> 00:12:09.674
make it so like no family in the country

00:12:09.674 --> 00:12:11.304
does not have sufficient internet access,

00:12:11.304 --> 00:12:13.724
just like clean drinking, water
heating like you mentioned,

00:12:13.724 --> 00:12:15.124
my back of the envelope calculations,

00:12:15.124 --> 00:12:17.354
it's a 1% of the trillion,

00:12:17.354 --> 00:12:20.014
that we're throwing around
a stimulus right now.

00:12:20.014 --> 00:12:21.314
And it seems like everyone's behind it.

00:12:21.314 --> 00:12:23.754
So what takes us across the finish line?

00:12:23.754 --> 00:12:26.444
- Yeah, what would always, I
guess, get both gives me hope,

00:12:26.444 --> 00:12:28.624
but frankly, infuriates me is Sal.

00:12:28.624 --> 00:12:30.344
This is not like a cure for cancer.

00:12:30.344 --> 00:12:32.424
This is not putting a man on Mars.

00:12:32.424 --> 00:12:34.624
This is simply a lack of vision.

00:12:34.624 --> 00:12:37.174
A lack of political
will, a lack of courage

00:12:37.174 --> 00:12:39.724
and frankly, a lack of caring
about our nation's kids.

00:12:39.724 --> 00:12:43.644
So the amount of money would
be a couple of billion dollars,

00:12:43.644 --> 00:12:45.674
whatever we'll figure
out what that number is.

00:12:45.674 --> 00:12:47.424
It is not overwhelming.

00:12:47.424 --> 00:12:50.404
You have the FCC and
E-Rate that has access

00:12:50.404 --> 00:12:52.114
to billions of dollars that could be used

00:12:52.114 --> 00:12:53.366
in a different way.

00:12:53.366 --> 00:12:55.674
And so the, the funding
issue here to your point

00:12:55.674 --> 00:12:57.534
is basically a rounding era.

00:12:57.534 --> 00:12:59.754
It's just believing in
kids and understanding

00:12:59.754 --> 00:13:02.504
that we need help every
child, wherever they are,

00:13:02.504 --> 00:13:05.294
find their genius, find
what they love to do.

00:13:05.294 --> 00:13:07.374
Find what they want to
do with their lives.

00:13:07.374 --> 00:13:11.074
And I say all the time that
talent is evenly distributed

00:13:11.074 --> 00:13:13.324
in our country, opportunity is not,

00:13:13.324 --> 00:13:15.084
we've got to close the opportunity gap.

00:13:15.084 --> 00:13:17.804
We've got to make sure
opportunity meets that talent

00:13:17.804 --> 00:13:20.197
wherever and everywhere that it is.

00:13:21.697 --> 00:13:24.244
- Arne in terms of federal levers,

00:13:24.244 --> 00:13:25.784
cause this is something
know I'm always told.

00:13:25.784 --> 00:13:28.864
And my understanding is that
most of education spending

00:13:28.864 --> 00:13:31.434
is happening at the state level,

00:13:31.434 --> 00:13:33.484
but clearly we have a federal
department of education.

00:13:33.484 --> 00:13:35.644
And it seems like for things like to get

00:13:35.644 --> 00:13:37.064
digital divide funds, which right.

00:13:37.064 --> 00:13:38.154
I mean, my back of the envelope,

00:13:38.154 --> 00:13:40.104
it's at most 10 $20 billion,

00:13:40.104 --> 00:13:42.544
which is a large number
for individual people,

00:13:42.544 --> 00:13:45.984
but it is a small percentage
of the stimulus type numbers

00:13:45.984 --> 00:13:47.304
that have been going out there.

00:13:47.304 --> 00:13:49.784
That seems like an obvious
thing where the federal

00:13:49.784 --> 00:13:51.984
department of education
could play a big role.

00:13:51.984 --> 00:13:54.574
What about these other things
that you're talking about?

00:13:54.574 --> 00:13:57.074
Allow changing the school
year, which I agree,

00:13:57.074 --> 00:13:59.104
full year's full day schooling,

00:13:59.104 --> 00:14:01.164
having tutoring programs,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:01.164 --> 00:14:04.677
What is an, I'm not sure
if we lost Arne just now.

00:14:05.574 --> 00:14:08.154
I don't, I'm not seeing him anymore.

00:14:08.154 --> 00:14:10.254
Well, I'll keep asking the question.

00:14:10.254 --> 00:14:13.274
What are the federal levers
that we have for that?

00:14:13.274 --> 00:14:17.394
Maybe we just have such a good question.

00:14:17.394 --> 00:14:21.384
Well, y'all are seeing the roast things

00:14:21.384 --> 00:14:26.384
that can go wrong when you're
doing distance communication.

00:14:26.524 --> 00:14:27.497
- I'm back, I'm back.

00:14:27.497 --> 00:14:28.330
- There you go.

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.344
You're really, did you get my question?

00:14:32.344 --> 00:14:33.244
- No, sorry, you cut out.

00:14:33.244 --> 00:14:34.954
I couldn't hear you, I apologize.

00:14:34.954 --> 00:14:38.274
- My bad, well, we'll
blame the internet again.

00:14:38.274 --> 00:14:40.894
The question was digital divide.

00:14:40.894 --> 00:14:43.724
That seems like a no brainer
from a federal department

00:14:43.724 --> 00:14:44.834
of education, point of view.

00:14:44.834 --> 00:14:47.434
It's a small percentage
of the multi trillions

00:14:47.434 --> 00:14:49.544
of stimulus that we're putting out there.

00:14:49.544 --> 00:14:51.014
These other things that you talked about,

00:14:51.014 --> 00:14:53.654
like the school day, the calendar,

00:14:53.654 --> 00:14:56.974
allowing for competency-based learn.

00:14:56.974 --> 00:14:57.807
I don't think you said it,

00:14:57.807 --> 00:14:59.694
but I've heard you say other
times say things like that

00:14:59.694 --> 00:15:03.964
or a tutoring program, can that
happen at the federal level,

00:15:03.964 --> 00:15:05.554
or is that something
that kind of the federal

00:15:05.554 --> 00:15:07.584
can influence the States to do?

00:15:07.584 --> 00:15:09.594
- Yeah, both of those, all of the above.

00:15:09.594 --> 00:15:11.074
So let's sort of take it one at a time.

00:15:11.074 --> 00:15:14.364
So for me, the E-Rate, the E-Rate program,

00:15:14.364 --> 00:15:16.404
that's a federal program,
that's at the national level.

00:15:16.404 --> 00:15:18.514
And again, this is a national crisis.

00:15:18.514 --> 00:15:20.754
It has to be a national initiative.

00:15:20.754 --> 00:15:22.474
So you do that.

00:15:22.474 --> 00:15:24.034
Closing the digital divide.

00:15:24.034 --> 00:15:26.814
We can't leave that locality by locality

00:15:26.814 --> 00:15:28.604
community by community, state by state.

00:15:28.604 --> 00:15:31.194
We need to do that for every
kid now, why are we waiting?

00:15:31.194 --> 00:15:32.514
What are we waiting for?

00:15:32.514 --> 00:15:34.644
So that leadership has to happen there.

00:15:34.644 --> 00:15:37.474
I think, is something like
a national tutoring program

00:15:37.474 --> 00:15:39.724
and what you're getting going in terms

00:15:39.724 --> 00:15:42.574
of the volunteer initiative
is extraordinarily important.

00:15:42.574 --> 00:15:45.534
I just wanna help you do
that and see that scale,

00:15:45.534 --> 00:15:47.824
that leadership come
with the federal level,

00:15:47.824 --> 00:15:51.014
more resources for pre-K that
can come at the federal level.

00:15:51.014 --> 00:15:52.834
We invested during the
Obama administration,

00:15:52.834 --> 00:15:55.109
more than a billion dollars in pre-K

00:15:55.109 --> 00:15:56.834
is one of the things I was most proud of.

00:15:56.834 --> 00:15:58.154
We just need to do more of that and work

00:15:58.154 --> 00:15:59.264
with Congress to do that.

00:15:59.264 --> 00:16:01.764
Some of the stuff, as you
said, is very, very local

00:16:01.764 --> 00:16:05.034
thinking differently about
the calendar, the school day,

00:16:05.034 --> 00:16:09.634
the school week, summers,
thinking about a pre-K through 14

00:16:09.634 --> 00:16:12.784
model for education in America,
rather than K through 12.

00:16:12.784 --> 00:16:15.324
Those are things that
absolutely would need to happen

00:16:15.324 --> 00:16:18.284
at the local level, but what
the federal government could do

00:16:18.284 --> 00:16:20.774
is they could provide
resources to help people

00:16:20.774 --> 00:16:23.464
think about those things,
help them model it,

00:16:23.464 --> 00:16:26.084
try and scale it, learn
what works, what doesn't,

00:16:26.084 --> 00:16:28.784
you have an amazing
opportunity always to convene

00:16:28.784 --> 00:16:30.174
at the federal level,

00:16:30.174 --> 00:16:33.691
to put resources behind
innovation and creativity.

00:16:33.691 --> 00:16:35.684
And so, again, whether it's hard dollars

00:16:35.684 --> 00:16:38.994
in some of this stuff or
just leadership and vision

00:16:38.994 --> 00:16:39.854
at the federal level,

00:16:39.854 --> 00:16:43.514
that you can play an
extraordinarily important part

00:16:43.514 --> 00:16:45.414
in helping give kids a chance in life.

00:16:46.834 --> 00:16:48.004
- Now, that makes a lot of sense.

00:16:48.004 --> 00:16:50.734
And we're, I mean, told by
our technical team, Arne,

00:16:50.734 --> 00:16:52.724
if you just touch your
screen every now and then

00:16:52.724 --> 00:16:53.994
it might be because your iPad

00:16:53.994 --> 00:16:55.714
is going into screensaver mode.

00:16:55.714 --> 00:16:58.694
So just, give it a little
love every every few minutes

00:16:59.664 --> 00:17:00.497
it'll be good.

00:17:00.497 --> 00:17:03.104
So we're getting a ton of questions.

00:17:03.104 --> 00:17:07.324
There's a question from
Scott Yang saying, yo Sal,

00:17:07.324 --> 00:17:09.334
when can things return to normal

00:17:09.334 --> 00:17:11.684
and I'm going to extend it 'cause I think,

00:17:11.684 --> 00:17:15.444
Scott is referring to normal
in the school system broadly.

00:17:15.444 --> 00:17:18.444
And obviously you referred
to this the notion

00:17:18.444 --> 00:17:21.064
of teachers aren't feeling
safe, fully protected.

00:17:21.064 --> 00:17:23.294
We clearly don't have a vaccine out there.

00:17:23.294 --> 00:17:26.794
What are you hearing as the best practices

00:17:26.794 --> 00:17:29.524
that school districts are
doing to navigate these times?

00:17:29.524 --> 00:17:30.624
Some people are fully open.

00:17:30.624 --> 00:17:33.234
Some people aren't, some
people are kind of hybrid.

00:17:33.234 --> 00:17:35.314
What would you advise the
school district right now

00:17:35.314 --> 00:17:36.427
to get up and running?

00:17:37.864 --> 00:17:38.697
- Couple of thoughts.

00:17:38.697 --> 00:17:40.224
First of all, school districts,

00:17:40.224 --> 00:17:42.414
schools, they're not
islands, they're not bubbles.

00:17:42.414 --> 00:17:43.904
They exist in community.

00:17:43.904 --> 00:17:46.374
And so the best thing, if
we truly care about kids,

00:17:46.374 --> 00:17:49.314
the best thing we can do
is to beat down this virus

00:17:49.314 --> 00:17:50.624
in our communities.

00:17:50.624 --> 00:17:52.534
And we have not done a good job of that.

00:17:52.534 --> 00:17:54.184
We've lacked discipline.

00:17:54.184 --> 00:17:56.324
We've opened bars before
we opened schools,

00:17:56.324 --> 00:17:57.704
which didn't make any sense.

00:17:57.704 --> 00:17:59.704
I think the way we have to
think about this has gotta

00:17:59.704 --> 00:18:03.524
be public health first,
public education second,

00:18:03.524 --> 00:18:05.274
and then reopening the economy third,

00:18:05.274 --> 00:18:06.914
it's gotta be in that
order, public health,

00:18:06.914 --> 00:18:09.444
public education, reopening economy.

00:18:09.444 --> 00:18:12.144
We've done that all backwards
with disastrous results.

00:18:12.144 --> 00:18:14.104
That's the most important thing we can do.

00:18:14.104 --> 00:18:18.724
The second thing I'll say is
that I think school districts

00:18:18.724 --> 00:18:20.994
in the absence of federal leadership

00:18:20.994 --> 00:18:24.624
have shown just unbelievable,
creativity in doing this,

00:18:24.624 --> 00:18:28.054
how you open carefully,
how you open slowly,

00:18:28.054 --> 00:18:30.534
how you open with the most
vulnerable children first

00:18:30.534 --> 00:18:32.294
or the youngest children's first,

00:18:32.294 --> 00:18:35.064
we're seeing fantastic
examples around the country.

00:18:35.064 --> 00:18:38.124
As you notice Sal, we
haven't seen big outbreaks,

00:18:38.124 --> 00:18:41.044
where districts are opening
up, they're doing it carefully.

00:18:41.044 --> 00:18:42.654
They're doing it thoughtfully.

00:18:42.654 --> 00:18:45.134
Kids are really adhering to wearing mask

00:18:45.134 --> 00:18:46.754
and social distancing.

00:18:46.754 --> 00:18:49.214
We've seen frankly, less of
that at the college level

00:18:49.214 --> 00:18:50.451
in higher education.

00:18:50.451 --> 00:18:52.844
And so schools, I think
are leading the way

00:18:52.844 --> 00:18:54.394
in terms of how we get there.

00:18:54.394 --> 00:18:55.774
The final point I'll make,

00:18:55.774 --> 00:18:57.934
and this is a really important one to me.

00:18:57.934 --> 00:19:00.244
I don't wanna go back to "normal"

00:19:00.244 --> 00:19:02.284
because normal didn't serve millions

00:19:02.284 --> 00:19:03.587
of children well enough.

00:19:04.434 --> 00:19:06.414
That's not a high enough bar.

00:19:06.414 --> 00:19:10.604
We have to use this time to
reimagine, reinvent education,

00:19:10.604 --> 00:19:14.394
do it through a lens of equity.

00:19:14.394 --> 00:19:15.674
What's fair, what's just,

00:19:15.674 --> 00:19:17.834
how do we close these opportunity gaps?

00:19:17.834 --> 00:19:20.334
How do we give every child
a chance to be successful?

00:19:20.334 --> 00:19:22.634
And we have to use this
very, very dark time

00:19:22.634 --> 00:19:25.504
to try and leapfrog to
something to be much better

00:19:25.504 --> 00:19:28.664
for our 53, 54 million
children across the country

00:19:28.664 --> 00:19:29.857
who're going to school.

00:19:30.954 --> 00:19:32.324
- Yeah, that makes sense.

00:19:32.324 --> 00:19:33.194
What's your sense?

00:19:33.194 --> 00:19:34.864
When we talked a lot of
districts, even the ones

00:19:34.864 --> 00:19:38.384
that have been able to mostly
close the digital divide,

00:19:38.384 --> 00:19:39.217
they're also talking,

00:19:39.217 --> 00:19:40.174
I have a school teacher who shares

00:19:40.174 --> 00:19:41.374
the cul de sac in our neighborhood.

00:19:41.374 --> 00:19:43.574
She teaches fifth grade and
this isn't a middle income,

00:19:43.574 --> 00:19:45.424
upper middle income neighborhood.

00:19:45.424 --> 00:19:47.834
She's telling me that in her class of 30

00:19:47.834 --> 00:19:50.254
there's two or three kids
that are just disengaged

00:19:50.254 --> 00:19:51.764
and she thinks they have internet access,

00:19:51.764 --> 00:19:54.294
but maybe they're don't
have the supports at home.

00:19:54.294 --> 00:19:57.094
And what we're hearing in
large urban school districts

00:19:57.094 --> 00:19:58.334
is the problems much, much worse.

00:19:58.334 --> 00:20:00.434
Even when there is internet access,

00:20:00.434 --> 00:20:03.774
how do we reach those
kids, or what do they need?

00:20:03.774 --> 00:20:05.684
What kind of support can we give them?

00:20:05.684 --> 00:20:07.374
- Yeah, that's so important.

00:20:07.374 --> 00:20:11.054
And let me say first that we
have so many in school systems

00:20:11.054 --> 00:20:12.334
and individual schools,

00:20:12.334 --> 00:20:14.254
you have so many
different responsibilities

00:20:14.254 --> 00:20:15.844
and it's not just the education part.

00:20:15.844 --> 00:20:17.574
Now I will come back and answer that.

00:20:17.574 --> 00:20:19.744
The first thing that schools
do across the country

00:20:19.744 --> 00:20:22.934
is they serve tens of millions of kids.

00:20:22.934 --> 00:20:25.234
Two, sometimes three meal a day.

00:20:25.234 --> 00:20:28.274
And throughout the pandemic,
starting in early March,

00:20:28.274 --> 00:20:30.374
we've done a weekly call
with school districts

00:20:30.374 --> 00:20:31.344
across the country

00:20:31.344 --> 00:20:34.254
who through all of this, as
physical buildings shut down,

00:20:34.254 --> 00:20:36.594
those schools continue to
be food distribution centers

00:20:36.594 --> 00:20:38.074
and drive throughs in front of it,

00:20:38.074 --> 00:20:41.784
just amazing leadership and
hard work to make that happen.

00:20:41.784 --> 00:20:42.914
Secondly, we have to take care

00:20:42.914 --> 00:20:44.824
of kids' social and emotional health.

00:20:44.824 --> 00:20:49.104
And so tele-health teachers,
counselors, psychologists,

00:20:49.104 --> 00:20:51.624
social workers, reaching out to children.

00:20:51.624 --> 00:20:54.144
Many of whom are dealing with
a lot of trauma before this.

00:20:54.144 --> 00:20:56.234
Many of whom are dealing
with unfortunately,

00:20:56.234 --> 00:20:58.764
a lot more trauma now, due to pandemic,

00:20:58.764 --> 00:21:02.584
due to economic insecurity,
due to systemic racism,

00:21:02.584 --> 00:21:05.864
due to food insecurity, we have
to take care of those kids.

00:21:05.864 --> 00:21:07.454
And then we get to the education part.

00:21:07.454 --> 00:21:09.984
So as we talk about, so
much, high tech and internet

00:21:09.984 --> 00:21:12.034
and broadband, all of that's great.

00:21:12.034 --> 00:21:13.914
But to answer your question directly,

00:21:13.914 --> 00:21:16.174
the most important thing
I think we can do now

00:21:16.174 --> 00:21:18.994
to reach those kids who
may have disappeared,

00:21:18.994 --> 00:21:19.967
who are struggling,

00:21:21.024 --> 00:21:22.294
we have to go old school,

00:21:22.294 --> 00:21:24.604
we have to go visit them at their homes.

00:21:24.604 --> 00:21:26.114
And it's been fascinating
with a young man.

00:21:26.114 --> 00:21:29.104
I worked with here in Chicago
trying to reduce violence

00:21:29.104 --> 00:21:30.834
on the South and West sides.

00:21:30.834 --> 00:21:33.634
The past few months, I spent
more time than I ever have

00:21:33.634 --> 00:21:37.167
on port steps and just
sitting and talking to guys,

00:21:38.004 --> 00:21:40.814
at their house, on their
block, in their neighborhood.

00:21:40.814 --> 00:21:43.624
I have to say, it's been an
amazing learning experience

00:21:43.624 --> 00:21:45.634
and just not sitting behind a desk,

00:21:45.634 --> 00:21:47.194
not having them come to our offices,

00:21:47.194 --> 00:21:48.434
although we're glad you'll slowly

00:21:48.434 --> 00:21:49.614
be able to have physical opposites,

00:21:49.614 --> 00:21:51.944
but just meeting them where
they are and listening

00:21:51.944 --> 00:21:54.134
and getting a sense of what's going on

00:21:54.134 --> 00:21:56.044
has been invaluable in building our trust

00:21:56.044 --> 00:21:57.654
and building our relationships.

00:21:57.654 --> 00:22:01.034
And so there's no substitute
for again, teachers,

00:22:01.034 --> 00:22:03.744
whoever is, again, maybe
not in the playbook

00:22:03.744 --> 00:22:04.704
in the traditional playbook,

00:22:04.704 --> 00:22:06.974
but this has done not
very nontraditional time.

00:22:06.974 --> 00:22:09.824
I would just strongly
urge, not just teachers,

00:22:09.824 --> 00:22:13.184
but educational leaders
to go find those kids,

00:22:13.184 --> 00:22:16.614
go to their homes, talk to
them, talk to their parents,

00:22:16.614 --> 00:22:18.474
find out what's going on,

00:22:18.474 --> 00:22:21.124
find out what we can do
to help them reengage.

00:22:21.124 --> 00:22:23.785
And I always say, when
kids are struggling,

00:22:23.785 --> 00:22:25.834
when they're not able to
participate in school,

00:22:25.834 --> 00:22:28.264
when their behavior isn't great

00:22:28.264 --> 00:22:30.394
for me, that's always
a symptom of a problem.

00:22:30.394 --> 00:22:32.714
The problem is much deeper
and we got to not deal

00:22:32.714 --> 00:22:34.084
with the symptoms here.

00:22:34.084 --> 00:22:36.984
We got to deal with the root
causes, whatever they may be.

00:22:38.364 --> 00:22:39.774
- Now, it makes a ton of sense.

00:22:39.774 --> 00:22:40.964
And you alluded to it.

00:22:40.964 --> 00:22:43.504
I mean, tell us a little
bit about the work

00:22:43.504 --> 00:22:45.047
that you're doing now at Cred.

00:22:46.254 --> 00:22:47.484
What is at work?

00:22:47.484 --> 00:22:51.274
Why have you kind of put your,
you could be doing anything

00:22:51.274 --> 00:22:52.374
in education or beyond.

00:22:52.374 --> 00:22:54.914
Why are you focusing your energies there?

00:22:54.914 --> 00:22:56.984
- Yeah, so I'm obsessed,

00:22:56.984 --> 00:22:59.794
just laser focused on reducing
gun violence in Chicago

00:22:59.794 --> 00:23:01.394
and I'll tell you.

00:23:01.394 --> 00:23:03.494
So I started to lose
friends to gun violence

00:23:03.494 --> 00:23:05.954
when I was a teen, I'm 55.

00:23:05.954 --> 00:23:08.054
And how so that's 40 years ago.

00:23:08.054 --> 00:23:10.614
Honestly, I think, when that happens,

00:23:10.614 --> 00:23:13.964
it shapes you and probably
scars you in some ways,

00:23:13.964 --> 00:23:16.034
a little bit difficult to talk about.

00:23:16.034 --> 00:23:18.174
During my time leading the
Chicago public schools,

00:23:18.174 --> 00:23:20.024
there's a whole bunch of
really, really proud of.

00:23:20.024 --> 00:23:21.704
We can talk about that all day,

00:23:21.704 --> 00:23:24.364
but on my watch during
my seven and a half years

00:23:24.364 --> 00:23:26.304
leading the Chicago
public schools on average,

00:23:26.304 --> 00:23:29.004
we had one of our children,
one of our students killed

00:23:29.004 --> 00:23:30.984
every two weeks due to gun violence.

00:23:30.984 --> 00:23:33.154
It was a staggering rate of loss

00:23:33.154 --> 00:23:35.654
and thank goodness, it
was never in school,

00:23:35.654 --> 00:23:38.504
but it was going home on the bus,

00:23:38.504 --> 00:23:40.264
walk into the corner store,

00:23:40.264 --> 00:23:43.404
shot through a living room
window, 7:30 in the morning,

00:23:43.404 --> 00:23:44.784
getting ready for school.

00:23:44.784 --> 00:23:47.864
And I just feel, I don't
feel, I know we as adults

00:23:47.864 --> 00:23:50.394
fail to keep our kids safe.

00:23:50.394 --> 00:23:54.314
And when our family moved to DC in 2009,

00:23:54.314 --> 00:23:56.524
very naively in hindsight,

00:23:56.524 --> 00:23:57.624
I thought we were rock bottom.

00:23:57.624 --> 00:23:59.464
I thought we couldn't get any
worse in terms of violence

00:23:59.464 --> 00:24:01.344
and for a whole a whole host of reasons,

00:24:01.344 --> 00:24:04.664
things got a lot worse than
seven years, we were in DC.

00:24:04.664 --> 00:24:08.684
So coming back home, Chicago
has given me every opportunity,

00:24:08.684 --> 00:24:10.884
educationally academically, athletically,

00:24:10.884 --> 00:24:13.144
socially, culturally, I
owe this city everything

00:24:13.144 --> 00:24:14.764
for me to come back here and not deal

00:24:14.764 --> 00:24:16.464
with this crisis didn't feel right.

00:24:16.464 --> 00:24:18.294
So we're just working
directly with a young men

00:24:18.294 --> 00:24:19.644
in the South and West sides,

00:24:19.644 --> 00:24:21.704
most likely to shoot and be shot

00:24:21.704 --> 00:24:23.894
and helping them move out of that life

00:24:23.894 --> 00:24:26.034
and start to be the real
strong community leaders

00:24:26.034 --> 00:24:28.614
they need to be to take our city

00:24:28.614 --> 00:24:30.684
to a much safer place.

00:24:30.684 --> 00:24:33.224
The level of fear and
trauma that our children

00:24:33.224 --> 00:24:37.594
are growing up with on the
Southwest side is unimaginable.

00:24:37.594 --> 00:24:38.894
And it's not right.

00:24:38.894 --> 00:24:40.524
It's not fair, it's not their fault.

00:24:40.524 --> 00:24:42.864
And we're trying to break those cycles.

00:24:42.864 --> 00:24:44.194
- Well, what do you
think is the major lever?

00:24:44.194 --> 00:24:46.024
I mean, you talked about
just that door to door work,

00:24:46.024 --> 00:24:48.174
making connections with young people.

00:24:48.174 --> 00:24:51.464
Is that really the core work
or are there other levers?

00:24:51.464 --> 00:24:54.044
- Yeah, this work is
very, very complicated,

00:24:54.044 --> 00:24:55.374
so I don't wanna ever oversimplify.

00:24:55.374 --> 00:24:56.304
So I'll just say there are five.

00:24:56.304 --> 00:24:58.274
What we call five pillars to our work.

00:24:58.274 --> 00:24:59.394
So one is street outreach.

00:24:59.394 --> 00:25:02.794
We have guys who formerly
were in the gangs

00:25:02.794 --> 00:25:04.284
and the cliques who have what we call

00:25:04.284 --> 00:25:08.244
LTO licensed to operate, who
they work to mediate conflicts.

00:25:08.244 --> 00:25:09.964
And actually it's crazy this past weekend,

00:25:09.964 --> 00:25:12.214
Saturday night, one of our
street outreach workers

00:25:12.214 --> 00:25:15.784
actually successfully
negotiated a hostile situation.

00:25:15.784 --> 00:25:18.284
The police stood down
and let him handle it

00:25:18.284 --> 00:25:19.864
because he had to let the relationship

00:25:19.864 --> 00:25:21.594
was unbelievable there.

00:25:21.594 --> 00:25:23.164
So we have street outreach that basically

00:25:23.164 --> 00:25:24.184
is our HR function.

00:25:24.184 --> 00:25:26.924
They bring young men into our program.

00:25:26.924 --> 00:25:30.074
Once guys come to us,
we do four other things.

00:25:30.074 --> 00:25:31.514
Every guy has a life coach.

00:25:31.514 --> 00:25:33.604
We always say experience
can be the best teacher,

00:25:33.604 --> 00:25:35.334
but doesn't have to be your experience.

00:25:35.334 --> 00:25:37.954
Our life coaches have lived
often very tough lives,

00:25:37.954 --> 00:25:40.384
but I've come out the back
end and want to give back

00:25:40.384 --> 00:25:42.884
and want to help out, they're remarkable.

00:25:42.884 --> 00:25:44.164
We have a clinical team.

00:25:44.164 --> 00:25:45.464
The amount of trauma our guys

00:25:45.464 --> 00:25:47.324
are dealing with is extraordinary.

00:25:47.324 --> 00:25:49.164
We see all the time that
hurt people, hurt people.

00:25:49.164 --> 00:25:51.094
We have to help guys heal and deal

00:25:51.094 --> 00:25:52.644
with a lifetime of trauma.

00:25:52.644 --> 00:25:55.334
We have an education team
with had well over a 100 guys,

00:25:55.334 --> 00:25:56.314
get high school diplomas.

00:25:56.314 --> 00:25:58.744
We have a set of folks
actually in college now,

00:25:58.744 --> 00:26:00.734
and it's been fantastic
to see that happen.

00:26:00.734 --> 00:26:02.554
And then we have a jobs
team and ultimately

00:26:02.554 --> 00:26:05.194
our goal is to move guys
out of the street economy,

00:26:05.194 --> 00:26:06.914
into the legal economy.

00:26:06.914 --> 00:26:09.764
And we have a jobs teams that
helps in that transition.

00:26:09.764 --> 00:26:13.354
Guys worked with us for nine
months, a year, 15 months.

00:26:13.354 --> 00:26:16.434
Now we have about four
dozen employers who hire

00:26:16.434 --> 00:26:17.524
at the back end.

00:26:17.524 --> 00:26:19.424
And so those are the five pillars

00:26:19.424 --> 00:26:24.004
that give us a chance to
give guys a chance in life.

00:26:24.004 --> 00:26:25.474
And I would say after a few

00:26:25.474 --> 00:26:28.687
we've given them not a second
chance, but a first chance.

00:26:29.684 --> 00:26:31.044
- Now, that's incredibly powerful,

00:26:31.044 --> 00:26:33.354
incredibly important work.

00:26:33.354 --> 00:26:34.234
One question here,

00:26:34.234 --> 00:26:36.134
I know we're running low
on time from Facebook,

00:26:36.134 --> 00:26:38.724
Carl James Mark Hart.

00:26:38.724 --> 00:26:40.794
It's about higher education, with the cost

00:26:40.794 --> 00:26:43.904
of university education in
the States at a record high

00:26:43.904 --> 00:26:45.764
and with COVID-19 demonstrating the extent

00:26:45.764 --> 00:26:47.744
to which university
education is self-taught

00:26:47.744 --> 00:26:49.854
when online resources
are readily available,

00:26:49.854 --> 00:26:52.504
how can the next government improve access

00:26:52.504 --> 00:26:55.034
to cost effective tertiary education,

00:26:55.034 --> 00:26:56.564
which lead to accredited qualifications,

00:26:56.564 --> 00:26:58.494
which are valued by employers?

00:26:58.494 --> 00:26:59.327
Big question

00:26:59.327 --> 00:27:02.854
- Yes, I think this pandemic is forcing us

00:27:02.854 --> 00:27:05.214
to be creative and think very
differently about education.

00:27:05.214 --> 00:27:07.374
So what can be delivered,

00:27:07.374 --> 00:27:09.434
not just what can be delivered online.

00:27:09.434 --> 00:27:12.304
Maybe what's even better
delivered more effectively,

00:27:12.304 --> 00:27:14.504
more efficiently delivered online.

00:27:14.504 --> 00:27:16.634
We're gonna come out of this much smarter

00:27:16.634 --> 00:27:20.094
than we went into this,
where, how we utilize

00:27:20.094 --> 00:27:22.084
our best teachers, whether it's K to 12,

00:27:22.084 --> 00:27:25.814
our best professors on
the higher education side,

00:27:25.814 --> 00:27:27.784
again, what you've done
and what you did for our,

00:27:27.784 --> 00:27:29.954
my kids said you're,
you're an amazing teacher.

00:27:29.954 --> 00:27:31.934
You didn't teach 25 kids in one class

00:27:31.934 --> 00:27:35.074
or 120 freshmen or sophomores.

00:27:35.074 --> 00:27:37.254
You've touched tens of millions of kids.

00:27:37.254 --> 00:27:40.644
And how do we create systems
where our best instructors

00:27:40.644 --> 00:27:42.674
in whatever it is, have more range,

00:27:42.674 --> 00:27:45.264
have more access to young people.

00:27:45.264 --> 00:27:46.314
Again, whether it's K to 12

00:27:46.314 --> 00:27:48.864
or in the on the higher end side.

00:27:48.864 --> 00:27:51.124
I think as I said earlier, as a country,

00:27:51.124 --> 00:27:54.444
we have to move to a pre-K to 14 model.

00:27:54.444 --> 00:27:57.144
Not everyone has to go to
a four year university,

00:27:57.144 --> 00:27:57.977
but I want to make sure

00:27:57.977 --> 00:28:00.304
they have the opportunity to do that.

00:28:00.304 --> 00:28:02.094
And so we just have to make
sure every young person

00:28:02.094 --> 00:28:04.704
coming out of high school
get four year university,

00:28:04.704 --> 00:28:07.584
two year community college,
technical vocational training

00:28:07.584 --> 00:28:09.764
that they have a plan beyond that.

00:28:09.764 --> 00:28:12.994
I think we will see more low cost,

00:28:12.994 --> 00:28:16.554
high quality models coming
out of higher education.

00:28:16.554 --> 00:28:19.044
Again, the federal government
could do a lot more

00:28:19.044 --> 00:28:22.204
to incentivize that and put
some resources behind that.

00:28:22.204 --> 00:28:23.881
And then the final thing
I would love to see

00:28:23.881 --> 00:28:25.254
the federal government do

00:28:25.254 --> 00:28:26.734
is where we're seeing universities.

00:28:26.734 --> 00:28:29.734
The goal for me is not to
just enroll young people

00:28:29.734 --> 00:28:31.734
is to have them graduate,
is to get that diploma.

00:28:31.734 --> 00:28:33.604
And the worst thing you can
have is all that college debt

00:28:33.604 --> 00:28:35.464
and no degree, that's the worst position.

00:28:35.464 --> 00:28:38.764
But if you graduate, you're
gonna be in pretty good shape.

00:28:38.764 --> 00:28:42.104
And so I think again,
at the federal level,

00:28:42.104 --> 00:28:45.554
we can incentivize those
universities are those new models

00:28:45.554 --> 00:28:48.784
where, whether it's
English language learners,

00:28:48.784 --> 00:28:50.094
whether it's Pell recipients,

00:28:50.094 --> 00:28:51.844
whether it's first
generation college goers

00:28:51.844 --> 00:28:54.274
or all of the above,
who's doing the best job,

00:28:54.274 --> 00:28:57.784
not just again of attracting
them, of enrolling them,

00:28:57.784 --> 00:28:59.834
but helping them graduate.

00:28:59.834 --> 00:29:01.544
I'd like to put a lot more resources

00:29:01.544 --> 00:29:03.714
behind those traditional institutions

00:29:03.714 --> 00:29:05.144
or new creative institutions

00:29:05.144 --> 00:29:07.874
that are emerging and
frankly, less resources behind

00:29:07.874 --> 00:29:10.524
those places that aren't as
serious about helping people

00:29:10.524 --> 00:29:13.494
get that piece of paper that's
so critically important.

00:29:13.494 --> 00:29:14.754
- Now, it's all about transparency.

00:29:14.754 --> 00:29:15.964
Not only are they graduating,

00:29:15.964 --> 00:29:17.884
but what are their job
placement, et cetera.

00:29:17.884 --> 00:29:18.944
So this next question,

00:29:18.944 --> 00:29:22.284
there has been a lot of
conversation on social media

00:29:22.284 --> 00:29:24.774
about your background.

00:29:24.774 --> 00:29:26.974
This is my serious question from Facebook,

00:29:26.974 --> 00:29:29.194
Ruth Brannigan asks, where is Arne?

00:29:29.194 --> 00:29:31.364
Was it his turn to bowl?

00:29:31.364 --> 00:29:32.197
I think that is in reference(mumbles).

00:29:32.197 --> 00:29:33.824
- Is it my turn to what?

00:29:33.824 --> 00:29:35.674
- Bowl, I think they think what's behind

00:29:35.674 --> 00:29:37.240
your are bowling shoes.

00:29:37.240 --> 00:29:39.907
(Arne laughing)

00:29:41.004 --> 00:29:43.364
That's like a nice bowling
alley, that's a nice view.

00:29:43.364 --> 00:29:44.197
- I don't wanna see any bowls.

00:29:44.197 --> 00:29:48.264
I'll tell you the folks
who's are kind enough

00:29:48.264 --> 00:29:50.234
to give our nonprofit office space here.

00:29:50.234 --> 00:29:52.647
It's actually an interesting story.

00:29:52.647 --> 00:29:57.544
They bronze the shoes of the
mentors of all the people

00:29:57.544 --> 00:30:00.034
who work at their company
and basically said,

00:30:00.034 --> 00:30:02.914
we built this company upon your shoulders.

00:30:02.914 --> 00:30:06.424
So they bronze the actual
shoes of the mentors

00:30:06.424 --> 00:30:11.414
of all of their employees
here so that the company

00:30:11.414 --> 00:30:12.564
gives you spaces Vistria

00:30:12.564 --> 00:30:14.264
and he has a pretty remarkable story.

00:30:14.264 --> 00:30:15.124
And people come visit.

00:30:15.124 --> 00:30:17.684
People often go show them
that the shoe of their mentor,

